1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,958
Last video ended on a number. Zero for sixteen. Zero

2
00:00:03,000 --> 00:00:06,000
real conflicts, out of sixteen flagged runs. This video, the

3
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:09,375
fix landed. Fifteen of those sixteen false positives, gone. But

4
00:00:09,375 --> 00:00:11,583
the test built to prove that fix actually works found

5
00:00:11,583 --> 00:00:14,417
something else instead. Two agents were given the exact same

6
00:00:14,417 --> 00:00:16,708
data. One of them didn't read it. It invented a

7
00:00:16,708 --> 00:00:19,333
different quarter for a filing that doesn't exist. It cited

8
00:00:19,333 --> 00:00:22,583
a source URL nobody gave it. This is a model

9
00:00:22,583 --> 00:00:25,208
that doesn't do the reading, not a comparator bug.

10
00:00:26,010 --> 00:00:29,093
Quick recap, for anyone new. Sixteen of thirty-one real runs

11
00:00:29,093 --> 00:00:32,302
this tool ever produced were false positives. Two agents were

12
00:00:32,302 --> 00:00:34,843
flagged as disagreeing when they'd simply been asked about different

13
00:00:34,843 --> 00:00:38,135
things. Zero were real conflicts. The diagnosis named two ways

14
00:00:38,135 --> 00:00:40,718
forward. Link each number to the concept it came from.

15
00:00:41,135 --> 00:00:44,177
Then the comparator stops comparing apples to someone else's oranges.

16
00:00:44,510 --> 00:00:46,843
Or actually test whether two agents can ever be shown

17
00:00:46,843 --> 00:00:50,052
the same fact. Nothing in thirty-one runs had put them

18
00:00:50,052 --> 00:00:53,093
in that position. One day. Both threads. In that order.

19
00:00:53,850 --> 00:00:56,308
The idea is simple to say. Every number a producer

20
00:00:56,475 --> 00:00:59,183
cites gets tagged with the concept it came from: Assets,

21
00:00:59,475 --> 00:01:02,725
Revenue, Diluted Earnings Per Share. If a number's concept could

22
00:01:02,725 --> 00:01:05,392
never plausibly come from the other producer, its absence there

23
00:01:05,392 --> 00:01:08,475
stops counting as a disagreement. The two producers' concept lists

24
00:01:08,475 --> 00:01:11,058
don't overlap at all, by design. So a tagged number

25
00:01:11,058 --> 00:01:14,808
is excluded from comparison entirely. Only the untagged ones, bare

26
00:01:14,850 --> 00:01:18,558
ratios with no label nearby, still get compared the old

27
00:01:18,558 --> 00:01:18,850
way.

28
00:01:19,620 --> 00:01:22,245
Three bugs surfaced before the numbers could be trusted. A

29
00:01:22,245 --> 00:01:24,870
decimal point inside a figure was mistaken for the end

30
00:01:24,870 --> 00:01:27,620
of a sentence. That hid a concept label sitting earlier

31
00:01:27,620 --> 00:01:31,120
in the same real sentence. The tagger only recognized natural-language

32
00:01:31,162 --> 00:01:34,537
phrasing, like "diluted EPS." It missed that the actual prompt

33
00:01:34,537 --> 00:01:38,662
format is the raw label itself, "EarningsPerShareDiluted," which models echo

34
00:01:38,662 --> 00:01:41,495
back verbatim. And distance to a label was measured from

35
00:01:41,495 --> 00:01:44,662
its start, not its end. That mis-matched two numbers named

36
00:01:44,662 --> 00:01:47,870
together in one sentence, with the closest label winning for

37
00:01:47,870 --> 00:01:50,912
the wrong one. All three were caught by tests before

38
00:01:50,912 --> 00:01:51,995
the measurement was trusted.

39
00:01:52,670 --> 00:01:54,962
Then the actual test. Not a new example built to

40
00:01:54,962 --> 00:01:57,962
prove the point. The sixteen real runs already sitting in

41
00:01:57,962 --> 00:02:01,087
last week's labeled record, the ones already flagged as false

42
00:02:01,087 --> 00:02:04,712
positives. Replay all sixteen through the new rule. Count what's

43
00:02:04,712 --> 00:02:04,920
left.

44
00:02:05,932 --> 00:02:08,640
Fifteen stop flagging. The sixteenth doesn't. That's the one where

45
00:02:08,640 --> 00:02:11,307
a number really was fabricated. It's supposed to still flag.

46
00:02:11,848 --> 00:02:14,682
Zero new false positives anywhere else in the corpus. Checked

47
00:02:14,682 --> 00:02:17,765
twice, not once. Once against the tagging logic by itself.

48
00:02:18,057 --> 00:02:20,723
Then again through the exact function the live comparison route

49
00:02:20,723 --> 00:02:24,432
actually calls. That confirms the wiring didn't quietly change the

50
00:02:24,432 --> 00:02:24,723
answer.

51
00:02:25,732 --> 00:02:29,357
Fifteen of sixteen real false positives, killed. The one survivor

52
00:02:29,357 --> 00:02:32,482
is the one confirmed real catch on record. It's the

53
00:02:32,482 --> 00:02:35,273
one fabricated number this tool has ever actually caught.

54
00:02:36,040 --> 00:02:38,790
Here's the test nothing in thirty-one runs had ever performed.

55
00:02:39,040 --> 00:02:42,123
Give both agents the exact same evidence. Not two different

56
00:02:42,123 --> 00:02:44,915
lenses on one company. The identical numbers. Then see if

57
00:02:44,915 --> 00:02:47,540
a real disagreement can even happen. Two local models. Same

58
00:02:47,540 --> 00:02:50,957
prompt, same data, same directive. Four runs. Apple twice, with

59
00:02:50,957 --> 00:02:53,123
the models swapped between roles to rule out a role

60
00:02:53,123 --> 00:02:55,457
effect. Then Microsoft. Then Nvidia.

61
00:02:56,200 --> 00:02:58,700
Four for four, flagged. The first time this flag has

62
00:02:58,700 --> 00:03:01,783
ever fired on evidence both sides actually saw. But not

63
00:03:01,783 --> 00:03:04,617
because the two models disagreed about a number. In three

64
00:03:04,617 --> 00:03:07,033
of the four runs, one model cited zero of the

65
00:03:07,033 --> 00:03:10,117
real figures. It invented a fiscal quarter that isn't in

66
00:03:10,117 --> 00:03:13,242
the data. It cited a source URL nobody gave it.

67
00:03:13,820 --> 00:03:15,903
In the fourth run, roles were swapped, so this isn't

68
00:03:15,903 --> 00:03:18,237
about which seat the model sat in. It did read

69
00:03:18,237 --> 00:03:20,695
the real numbers. It still turned a real one hundred

70
00:03:20,695 --> 00:03:23,903
one billion, four hundred sixty-four million dollars of net income

71
00:03:23,903 --> 00:03:26,570
into a "net income loss" of negative one hundred one

72
00:03:26,570 --> 00:03:29,862
million. Wrong sign. Wrong by a factor of a thousand.

73
00:03:30,610 --> 00:03:33,152
The other model got every real figure right, all four

74
00:03:33,152 --> 00:03:36,235
times. It just wasn't clean either. It also added a

75
00:03:36,610 --> 00:03:39,068
return-on-equity number that appears nowhere in what it was given.

76
00:03:39,443 --> 00:03:42,235
So the mechanism works. It found a real problem. Just

77
00:03:42,235 --> 00:03:43,693
not the one it was built to find.

78
00:03:44,460 --> 00:03:46,877
A second session, same day, turned the measurement into a

79
00:03:46,877 --> 00:03:50,543
decision: ship it. The live comparison route now runs the

80
00:03:50,918 --> 00:03:53,793
concept-aware check by default, not the old rule. Two things

81
00:03:53,793 --> 00:03:54,877
came along for free.

82
00:03:55,550 --> 00:03:58,050
First, the old rule had its own quiet failure. It

83
00:03:58,050 --> 00:04:00,342
required both agents to cite at least one number before

84
00:04:00,342 --> 00:04:03,467
flagging anything. That's what suppressed the one confirmed true positive

85
00:04:03,467 --> 00:04:05,592
in the first place. The new rule never had that

86
00:04:05,592 --> 00:04:08,508
requirement. Switching to it fixed a second, separate bug, with

87
00:04:08,508 --> 00:04:11,717
no extra code. Second: a real extraction bug, found while

88
00:04:11,717 --> 00:04:15,008
building last week's corpus. A large number with commas and

89
00:04:15,008 --> 00:04:17,508
no dollar sign was getting truncated to its last three

90
00:04:17,508 --> 00:04:22,092
digits. Thirteen billion, nine hundred seventy-one million dollars was extracting

91
00:04:22,092 --> 00:04:26,258
as the string zero-zero-zero point zero. It's fixed. It's

92
00:04:26,258 --> 00:04:29,217
confirmed against the real historical case, not just a new

93
00:04:29,217 --> 00:04:30,383
example built to pass.

94
00:04:31,180 --> 00:04:34,263
And the fabrication check that already existed for single-agent chats

95
00:04:34,263 --> 00:04:37,388
had never once been connected to this comparison route. It

96
00:04:37,388 --> 00:04:39,888
extracts every claim a model makes. It checks each one

97
00:04:39,888 --> 00:04:43,013
against its own cited source. It is now connected. Replayed

98
00:04:43,013 --> 00:04:46,055
against the exact historic fabrication, not a clean example. The

99
00:04:46,055 --> 00:04:48,638
citation supports none of the numbers under it. A real,

100
00:04:48,638 --> 00:04:52,097
automatic signal. Not "this number is fake." It's "this source

101
00:04:52,097 --> 00:04:55,430
doesn't back what's claimed here." That's what actually caught it.

102
00:04:55,990 --> 00:04:58,365
True now that wasn't a week ago. Fifteen of sixteen

103
00:04:58,365 --> 00:05:01,782
real false positives, gone. Measured against every run this tool

104
00:05:01,782 --> 00:05:04,865
has ever produced, not four. Sixteen. The one suppressed true

105
00:05:04,865 --> 00:05:07,198
positive fires again, as a side effect of the same

106
00:05:07,198 --> 00:05:10,865
fix. A comma-formatted number extracts correctly instead of losing everything

107
00:05:10,865 --> 00:05:14,073
but its last three digits. Claim verification runs on this

108
00:05:14,073 --> 00:05:16,782
comparison route for the first time. It produces a real

109
00:05:16,782 --> 00:05:20,157
signal against the one case that matters. Four ledger issues

110
00:05:20,157 --> 00:05:23,532
closed in one day. The comparison route has automated tests

111
00:05:23,532 --> 00:05:26,198
for the first time ever, three of them. Two hundred

112
00:05:26,240 --> 00:05:29,365
forty-two tests, up from two hundred twenty-four, green at every

113
00:05:29,365 --> 00:05:29,740
step.

114
00:05:30,360 --> 00:05:33,152
Still not true. A legitimate ratio and a fabricated one

115
00:05:33,152 --> 00:05:36,193
still look identical to this system. Both are just numbers

116
00:05:36,193 --> 00:05:39,318
with no label nearby. Narrowing that gap from "any disconnected

117
00:05:39,318 --> 00:05:42,443
number" to "any unlabeled one" does not close it. Whatever's

118
00:05:42,443 --> 00:05:44,568
actually wrong with one of the two local models used

119
00:05:44,568 --> 00:05:47,193
in this test hasn't been investigated. Maybe it's reading its

120
00:05:47,193 --> 00:05:50,152
own input. Maybe it's a specific quantization. Maybe it's this

121
00:05:50,152 --> 00:05:53,402
prompt shape. It's flagged, not fixed. It's a different kind

122
00:05:53,402 --> 00:05:55,818
of problem than anything this tool was designed to catch.

123
00:05:56,360 --> 00:05:59,318
The comparison route has three tests now. Every other route,

124
00:05:59,318 --> 00:06:02,110
and every line of interface code, still has zero. The

125
00:06:02,110 --> 00:06:05,110
four critical security findings from the original audit are exactly

126
00:06:05,110 --> 00:06:07,152
as open as they were. And the pile of work

127
00:06:07,152 --> 00:06:10,860
sitting outside version control got bigger again. Eleven more files,

128
00:06:10,860 --> 00:06:13,443
on top of everything already sitting there uncommitted.

129
00:06:14,050 --> 00:06:16,508
So here's the smallest true claim. The tool got better

130
00:06:16,508 --> 00:06:19,508
at not crying wolf. Fifteen fewer false alarms, for free,

131
00:06:19,592 --> 00:06:21,758
the same day they were measured. That's real. It did

132
00:06:21,758 --> 00:06:23,592
not get better at telling a real number from a

133
00:06:23,592 --> 00:06:26,758
convincing fake one. Those are different problems. A comparator that

134
00:06:26,758 --> 00:06:30,175
stops over-flagging is one thing. A comparator that catches fabrication

135
00:06:30,175 --> 00:06:34,217
is another. Zero-for-sixteen got solved for one reason, and stayed

136
00:06:34,217 --> 00:06:37,550
unsolved for the other. Mixing those two up is exactly

137
00:06:37,550 --> 00:06:41,467
the overclaim this format exists to catch. Fifteen of sixteen.

138
00:06:41,508 --> 00:06:44,175
One left. And one model that, this week, showed it

139
00:06:44,175 --> 00:06:46,008
can't be trusted to read its own homework.

140
00:06:47,063 --> 00:06:50,813
Fifteen of sixteen. Written down, versioned, and executable. Signing off,

141
00:06:50,813 --> 00:06:51,313
Divij Pawar.
